<description>
Connect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then emit perpendicular rays from differently-colored pixels on those diagonals.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Line color (e.g. blue) has pixel pairs aligned on shared diagonals. Trigger color (e.g. magenta) pixels sit along those diagonals.

Rule:
- Draw diagonal lines connecting each pair of same-color aligned pixels.
- Where the diagonal crosses a trigger pixel, emit perpendicular diagonal rays (in the trigger's color) extending to the grid boundary.

Key insight: Trigger pixels act as beam-splitters — the perpendicular rays rotate 90° from the connecting diagonal's orientation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="87ab05b8" lines="9-24">
<description>
Erase all distractor pixels and expand the single red control pixel into the grid quadrant that contains it.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- Exactly one red pixel is the control pixel.
- Other non-background pixels are distractors.

Rule:
- Clear the canvas to the background color.
- Split the grid into equal top/bottom and left/right halves.
- Fill the quadrant containing the red control pixel with red.

Key insight:
- The red control pixel selects a whole half-grid quadrant by its row half and column half; all other foreground pixels are noise.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="e4888269" lines="67-88">
<description>
Recolor every target pixel by following the ordered two-column substitution legend.

Input structure
- A dominant background surrounds one multicolor vertical legend: a connected,
  fully filled two-column block with one color pair per row.
- Other non-background pixels or objects are the targets; divider or frame
  pixels are ordinary target-side structure that remain unchanged unless their
  color is rewritten by the legend.

Rule
- Read the legend rows from top to bottom as substitutions from the left
  column color to the right column color.
- For each non-background pixel outside the legend, scan those rows in order;
  whenever the current color equals the row's left color, replace it with the
  row's right color and keep scanning lower rows.
- Leave the legend itself and background pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The same color may be rewritten multiple times, but only by substitutions
  that appear later in the vertical legend, so the row order is part of the
  symbol instruction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="dc46ea44" lines="11-25">
<description>
Move the lower sprite and its pink guide into the upper panel, aligning the sprite to the guide's lower-left branch.

Input structure
- The grid has one dominant background color and one full-width separator row.
- Below the separator are exactly two foreground roles: a five-pixel guide marker and one monochrome sprite.
- The guide marker has normalized shape {(0,2), (1,2), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1)} and starts immediately below the separator.

Rule
- Clear the canvas to the background, preserving the separator row.
- Translate the guide marker upward so its top row becomes row 0.
- Move the sprite, preserving its color and shape, so the sprite bbox bottom-right aligns to the translated guide's lower-left branch.

Key insight
- The marker supplies the destination anchor: its lower-left branch cell is where the moved sprite's bbox bottom-right must land.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="edcc2ff0" lines="47-63">
<description>
Keep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.

Input structure
A black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers
in the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has
a unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.

Rule
In the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground
object to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0
whose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.

Key insight
The top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects
survive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="42f14c03" lines="75-83">
<description>
Crop the foreground object whose missing cells match marker shapes, and fill those missing cells with the marker colors.

Input structure
A dominant background surrounds several monochromatic foreground objects. Exactly one object has background gaps inside its bounding box whose component shapes are matched by separate marker objects.
Rule
Find that object, crop its bounding box, and replace each background gap component with the color of the separate marker component or components having the same normalized shape.
Key insight
The marker objects do not mark absolute positions; their shapes identify which gaps in the selected object should be colored, while objects with unmatched gaps are distractors.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="1a244afd" lines="49-68">
<description>
Rotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.
- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same
  row or column.

Rule:
- Remove all magenta source pixels.
- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the
  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.
- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the
  grid, the source simply disappears.

Key insight:
The blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a
unique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="18286ef8" lines="17-31">
<description>
Use the pink control pixel to choose where the maroon pixel belongs inside the grey 3x3 region, and turn the control pixel maroon.

Input structure:
- The grid contains one 3x3 region made from eight grey pixels and one maroon pixel.
- There is exactly one pink control pixel outside that region.

Rule:
- Restore the old maroon cell in the 3x3 region to grey.
- Compare the pink pixel to the 3x3 region: above/same band/below selects top/middle/bottom, and left/same band/right selects left/middle/right.
- Paint the selected cell maroon and recolor the pink control pixel maroon.

Key insight:
- The pink pixel acts as a relative-position marker for a 3x3 target inside the grey region.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="1efba499" lines="70-85">
<description>
Swap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.
- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.

Rule:
- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.
- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.
- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.

Key insight:
- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="963c33f8" lines="86-108">
<description>
Drop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.
- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows
  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.

Rule
- Erase the top marker.
- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.
- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the
  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.
- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;
  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the
  run one column right before bottom-aligning.
- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear
  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.

Key insight
- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean
  "stick to the first obstacle," while maroon cells mean "fall through."
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="984d8a3e" lines="5-21">
<description>
Shift each row's dominant middle-color run left until its right edge aligns with the leftmost original right edge, moving displaced left-side pixels after the run.

Input structure:
- The grid has three colors.
- The most common color forms one contiguous horizontal run in every row.
- Pixels before those runs share one side color, and pixels after those runs share the other side color.

Rule:
- Find the minimum right edge column among the middle-color runs.
- In each row, keep the run width and the trailing right-side color count.
- Place the middle-color run so its right edge is at that minimum column, unless the run is too wide, in which case start it at column 0.
- Fill any remaining non-trailing cells with the left-side color.

Key insight:
The varying row prefixes are movable padding; the stable feature is the leftmost right boundary of the middle-color shape.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="8b7bacbf" lines="42-56">
<description>
Fill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.
- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.

Rule:
- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.
- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.
- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.

Key insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="c4d1a9ae" lines="28-48">
<description>
Fill each vertical foreground band's background cells with the color of the next band to its right.

Input structure
- The dominant color is the background.
- Each other color occupies a separate vertical band defined by that color's
  minimum and maximum columns.
- Within a band, all cells are either that band's color or background.

Rule
- Order the bands from left to right.
- In each band, replace background pixels with the color of the next band in
  that order, wrapping from the rightmost band back to the leftmost.

